    "executiveSummary": "Think of human emotions like an automobile dashboard. When someone cuts you off in traffic or a project deadline collapses at work, the situation itself is simply a pothole in the road. It does not control your steering wheel. Albert Ellis reveals that rage is never caused by external events, but by an internal cognitive distortion where a reasonable personal desire ('I want this commute to be smooth') gets hardwired into an absolute universal demand ('The highway must accommodate me!'). That dogmatic 'must' is the spark plug that detonates destructive fury.\n\nLike an overzealous home electrical breaker that trips and cuts the power every time the toaster runs, an uncontrolled nervous system treats everyday friction like a lethal threat. Traditional advice often urges people to either suppress the feeling or vent it into a punching bag. Ellis demonstrates that venting rage does not drain the battery; it merely conditions the brain to repeat the pattern. Real mastery requires inspecting the wiring behind the wall: auditing the rigid beliefs, unmasking low frustration tolerance, and dismantling the illusion that the universe owes anyone flawless cooperation.\n\nBy systematically upgrading dogmatic demands into flexible preferences, you transform boiling hostility into clean, focused energy. When a garden encounters unexpected frost, cursing the weather yields nothing; insulating the soil preserves the harvest. Shifting from toxic anger to healthy annoyance keeps your mental engine running cool, preserves your physical health, and returns full authority over your emotional state.",
