      "chapterNumber": 1,
      "headline": "Must You Feel Angry?",
      "keyInsight": "Most people treat anger like a sudden rainstorm. They think it pours directly onto them from the sky. If someone cuts you off in traffic, anger feels automatic. But that is an optical illusion. Think of a spicy pepper. The spice does not burn your skin by just sitting on the table. You have to take a bite and chew it. In the exact same way, bad events never inject anger into your veins. You cook it up yourself in your own mental kitchen. You always have an emotional spice knob in your hands. You can choose mild annoyance instead of a five-alarm wildfire. Annoyance gives you energy to fix a problem. Wild fury just burns your own dinner and leaves you coughing in the smoke.",
      "content": "Imagine you are driving down a quiet street on a sunny afternoon. Suddenly, a blue sedan zooms past and swerves right into your lane. You slam on the brakes, your coffee spills, and your chest turns hot. Most of us instantly yell, \"That driver made me so mad!\" It feels like an arrow shot straight into your heart. But pause the camera right there. Did the blue car reach through your windshield and pump adrenaline into your bloodstream? It did not. The car just moved across asphalt. What triggered the explosion was a split-second story running through your head: \"Nobody should ever cut in front of me!\"\n\nHere is the great lab experiment of life. Put ten different drivers in that exact same situation. One driver screams and honks until their veins bulge. Another sighs, shakes their head, and turns up the radio. A third driver smiles and thinks, \"Wow, that person must be rushing to a hospital.\" The external event was identical for all three people. Yet the emotional results were completely different. That proves the event itself does not carry anger. The reaction depends entirely on what lens you snap onto your eyes.\n\nAlbert Ellis points out a huge difference between healthy annoyance and destructive rage. Healthy annoyance says, \"I really dislike this traffic jam, so let me find a detour.\" Destructive rage says, \"This road must clear for me right now!\" Annoyance keeps your hands steady on the steering wheel. Rage causes you to tailgate, smash your horn, and risk an accident. You can care deeply about fairness without torching your own peace of mind. You never have to hand your steering wheel over to pure rage.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Outside events never create your anger; your internal thoughts hold the master switch.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Traffic jams and rude strangers cannot inject anger into your body. You create your feelings based on the story you tell yourself.",
