      "headline": "How You Create Your Own Anger: The ABCs of REBT",
      "keyInsight": "We all carry an invisible factory inside our heads that builds our emotions. Most folks believe point A (the bad event) leads directly to point C (the angry blowup). A flat tire equals instant fury. But that skips the secret engine in the middle: point B. Point B stands for your personal Beliefs. A flat tire is just rubber with a hole in it. The tire has zero feelings. Point B is your inner voice barking, \"My day is totally ruined, and flat tires must never happen to me!\" The magic secret is that B does all the heavy lifting. Change the belief from a strict command to a soft preference, and point C magically transforms from explosive rage into calm problem-solving.",
      "content": "Think of your mind like a bakery. Point A is a bag of plain raw flour dropped on your counter. That represents an event in your life: a delayed flight, a noisy neighbor, or a spilled mug. Raw flour cannot bake a pie by itself. It just sits there on the counter. Point B is the chef's recipe book. That is what you believe about the event. If your recipe says, \"Turn every mess into a firestorm,\" you mix in boiling hot spices. Point C is the finished pie on the table: your emotional explosion.\n\nMost people point at the flour and scream, \"The flour burnt my mouth!\" That sounds absurd when you say it out loud. Yet we do it every single day. We blame the delayed flight for our screaming fit at the boarding gate. In reality, the flight delay was just the raw ingredient. Your belief was the blender: \"The airline should never make a mistake, and I cannot stand waiting!\" That hidden demand creates every drop of bile you taste.\n\nAlbert Ellis showed that we lock ourselves up with rigid words like \"must,\" \"should,\" and \"have to.\" When you change those demands into simple preferences, everything shifts. Try saying, \"I really prefer on-time flights, but delays happen sometimes.\" Notice what happens to your shoulders. They drop. Your jaw unclenches. You still want the plane to arrive, but you stop acting like the sky is falling. You regain complete control of the kitchen.",
      "keyTakeaway": "You do not react to facts; you react to the rigid rules you paste over those facts.",
