      "id": "overcoming-destructive-anger-ch-4",
      "order": 4,
      "chapterNumber": 4,
      "headline": "Hunting the Hidden Rules in Your Head",
      "keyInsight": "Here is the weird trick your brain plays on you every single day. When someone cuts you off in traffic, you swear their car caused your rage. But notice what really happened. The car merely tapped the brakes. Your brain instantly opened a secret filing cabinet and pulled out an unwritten universal law: 'Drivers must never inconvenience me!' The other driver did not build your fury. You manufactured it in three seconds flat using your own secret rulebook. We walk around carrying invisible manuals for how the universe should treat us. Whenever reality violates page forty-two of your secret rulebook, alarm bells scream. You feel like a victim of bad luck or rude people. In truth, you are merely tripping over tripwires you strung up across your own mental living room.",
      "content": "Imagine you hire an interior decorator to arrange your house. Without telling you, they install a spring-loaded trapdoor in the hallway. Every time someone steps on a squeaky floorboard, the trapdoor drops them into a pit of hot sauce. You would fire that decorator immediately! Yet this is exactly how we set up our emotional architecture. We scatter invisible trigger plates everywhere. We demand that line cooks never burn toast, trains never arrive late, and coworkers always reply instantly.\n\nWhen you study anger like a field scientist, you discover something fascinating. Events carry zero emotional temperature by themselves. A rainstorm on your wedding day is just drops of water hitting the soil. It feels miserable only because you brought a silent demand: 'The sky must be completely clear today!' The rain cannot obey you because clouds do not read your mind. Anger begins the very second you convert a simple preference into a rigid law of physics.\n\nNext time your blood starts boiling, stop looking at the person in front of you. Point your flashlight backward into your own mind instead. Ask yourself: 'What silly unwritten rule did I just write into the universe's code?' You will almost always spot a big, shiny, bossy sign that says 'People must always treat me nicely.' Once you spot that ridiculous sign, the mystery of your anger disappears.",
