      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Rewiring the Angry Mind",
      "keyInsight": "Here is the great mental trick: other people never make you furious. You do it to yourself. When someone cuts you off in traffic, your brain performs a secret translation. It turns a simple wish into an absolute rule. You whisper, \"They must never do that!\" That single demanding word acts like a spark inside dry leaves. If you simply say, \"I wish they drove better, but they do not have to,\" the explosion disappears. You are not forced to stay calm, but you stop setting your own house on fire. Demanding perfection from messy humans is like screaming at rain for being wet.",
      "content": "Imagine your brain running an invisible kitchen. Raw events arrive like cold groceries on the counter. A coworker misses a deadline, or a soup bowl spills on a clean rug. By themselves, cold groceries cannot burn your tongue. You have to cook them first. When you get mad, your brain tosses those groceries into a pressure cooker powered by rigid rules. You tell yourself that bad things must never happen. The steam builds instantly until the lid flies across the room.\n\nNow look at what happens when you swap that pressure cooker for an open pan. You look at the mess and state the plain truth. Spilling soup is annoying and messy, but the universe never promised clean floors. By questioning your stubborn demands, you cool the stove down. You replace rigid commands with sensible preferences. It turns an unbearable catastrophe into an ordinary chore that needs a paper towel.\n\nThink of it like driving with a cracked windshield. If you insist every road must be glass-smooth, every bump feels like an insult. But once you accept that potholes exist everywhere, you steer around them without grinding your teeth. You solve the problem instead of fighting the road.",
