      "chapterNumber": 8,
      "headline": "Cooling the Emotional Furnace",
      "keyInsight": "Most people think feelings are like wild weather that you must endure. But your emotions are more like indoor climate control. When someone wrongs you, you do not have to pick between blind fury and cold numbness. There is a sweet middle spot called healthy sorrow or annoyance. Healthy annoyance gives you energy to fix a problem without burning down your living room. Unhealthy fury blinds you, spikes your blood pressure, and makes you look ridiculous. You can feel deep disappointment while keeping total control of the thermostat.",
      "content": "Picture your feelings as water flowing through garden pipes. When something goes wrong, you naturally feel a surge of energy. If you shut the valve completely, the pressure bursts the pipe. But if you channel that water into a high-pressure firehose, you wash away your flowers and drown the lawn. Rage is that wild firehose. It wrecks the garden it was supposed to protect.\n\nHealthy emotion works like an adjustable garden nozzle. When someone treats you unfairly, you feel clean frustration. You do not pretend everything is fine. You admit that you dislike what happened. But instead of soaking everyone in boiling water, you use that steady stream to set clear boundaries. You fix the leak instead of smashing the plumbing with a wrench.\n\nThink about training a muscle at the gym. Straining until your joints snap does not make you strong. Focused, steady effort builds real power. By letting yourself feel honest disappointment without spiraling into revenge, your mind stays clear, steady, and sharp.",
      "keyTakeaway": "Aim for healthy disappointment rather than blind rage, so you can solve trouble without creating new damage.",
      "audioSnippetText": "Rage acts like a wild firehose that ruins your garden. Switch to healthy frustration to protect your boundaries without making a mess.",
